<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Appendix</h2>

<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1 </span>Marginal &amp; Joint CP Implementations</h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In observing the algorithms found in <a href="#alg3" title="Algorithm 3 ‣ 7.3 Noise Generation Implementation for 𝜉_𝑘 ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ Hybrid State Estimation of Uncertain Nonlinear Dynamics Using Neural Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#alg4" title="Algorithm 4 ‣ 7.3 Noise Generation Implementation for 𝜉_𝑘 ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ Hybrid State Estimation of Uncertain Nonlinear Dynamics Using Neural Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we demonstrate the computation of user-defined <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\alpha)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\alpha)</annotation></semantics></math> quantiles used to construct conformal set <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{\alpha}(x_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{\alpha}(x_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> in both the marginal and joint versions of this algorithm. We refer readers to Section <a href="#S4.SS4" title="4.4 Conformal Prediction for Calibrated Uncertainty Guarantees ‣ 4 Methodology ‣ Hybrid State Estimation of Uncertain Nonlinear Dynamics Using Neural Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> for more details on the conformal prediction algorithm. For both approaches, note that we always used a calibration set size <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}|=1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>cal</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}|=1000</annotation></semantics></math> after each epoch to ensure our computed quantile approximated the model’s true uncertainty to an adequate level. Pertaining to the marginal algorithm, it is important to note that in specifically computing <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\alpha}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\alpha}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math>, the quantity <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}^{(j)}[\left\lceil(N+1)(1-\alpha)\right\rceil]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⌉</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}^{(j)}[\left\lceil(N+1)(1-\alpha)\right\rceil]</annotation></semantics></math> seen in line 17 represents the <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left\lceil(N+1)(1-\alpha)\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left\lceil(N+1)(1-\alpha)\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>-th entry of the state-specific ordered array <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}^{(j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}^{(j)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Additionally, recall that <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=\text{state-dim}(y_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>state-dim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=\text{state-dim}(y_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, it is clear that in the joint version of the CP algorithm, we note that the conformity score computation in line 13 uses the entire state vector for both <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{y}_{T}^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{y}_{T}^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> to evaluate scalar quantile <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math>. In observing the conformity score computation, we note that <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\left(\sqrt{\hat{\sigma}_{T}^{2}}\cdot I\right)^{-1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\sqrt{\hat{\sigma}_{T}^{2}}\cdot I\right)^{-1}\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</annotation></semantics></math> represents an inverted identity matrix where each diagonal term is scaled by each model predicted standard deviation in <math id="S7.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\sigma}_{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\sigma}_{T}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2 </span>Real-Time Feasibility Discussion</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When observing the parameter counts of all models in Table <a href="#S7.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 7.2 Real-Time Feasibility Discussion ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ Hybrid State Estimation of Uncertain Nonlinear Dynamics Using Neural Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, it can be seen that the AttNP contains significantly less parameters in comparison to the baselines despite being a purely data-driven approach (note that physics model <math id="S7.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> does not increase parameter count of AttNP). It should also be noted that the PI-AttNP and other baselines all possess parameter counts low enough to generally be utilized in many forms of micro-processing hardware such as a Raspberry Pi, NVIDIA Jetson, etc <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Baller et al. (2021)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To further emphasize the feasibility of our models in a real-time setting, we record the compute time for each model’s forward pass of <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1,100,\;\text{and}\;1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1,100</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mtext>and</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 1000</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1,100,\;\text{and}\;1000</annotation></semantics></math> simultaneous samples, respectively. This simulation of parallel forward passes can be seen as a representative example of the compute time needed in multi-agent state estimation. As can be gathered from Table <a href="#S7.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 7.2 Real-Time Feasibility Discussion ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ Hybrid State Estimation of Uncertain Nonlinear Dynamics Using Neural Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, our PI-AttNP approach remains an attractive option in comparison to other baselines in computational efficiency. We observe that the PINN-LSTM generally provides the fastest single foward pass inference time across all batch sizes; however, with the incorporation of MC dropout, computation time will scale linearly by the number of forward passes used in computing the model’s predictive posterior - a computational bottleneck that is not required for the PI-AttNP. Pertaining to the DKF, we observe that due to the size of the model and recursive UKF algorithm utilized, computation times are considerably larger than other approaches. Note that all recorded times were executed on a computer with an 13th Gen Intel® Core™ i7-1360P × 16 with 32 GB of RAM.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T6" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S7.T6.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S7.T6.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S7.T6.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S7.T6.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">Models</span></th>
<th id="S7.T6.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S7.T6.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">Parameter Count</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S7.T6.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S7.T6.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_l ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S7.T6.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">PI-/AttNP</span></th>
<td id="S7.T6.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S7.T6.3.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:120%;">192408</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S7.T6.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
