<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3 </span>Noise Generation Implementation for <math id="S7.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Algorithm <a href="#alg5" title="Algorithm 5 ‣ 7.3 Noise Generation Implementation for 𝜉_𝑘 ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ Hybrid State Estimation of Uncertain Nonlinear Dynamics Using Neural Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, one can determine how multimodal Gaussian noise vector <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> is computed through a sampling mechanism and injected into <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with both additive/multiplicative components similar to other approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2023)</a></cite>. From the input arguments, we denote <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{max}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{max}}</annotation></semantics></math> the maximum number of peaks that can exist in <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{total}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{total}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the total number of sample points stored in arbitrary array <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{motion}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>motion</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{motion}}</annotation></semantics></math> being a small scalar used to ensure the incorporation of multiplicative effects from <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> remain within realistic bounds for typical motion sensors. Additionally, note that Gaussian noise parameter minimum/maximum values <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\xi(\text{min})},\;\mu_{\xi(\text{max})},\sigma_{\xi(\text{min})},\;\sigma_{\xi(\text{max})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>min</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>max</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>min</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>max</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\xi(\text{min})},\;\mu_{\xi(\text{max})},\sigma_{\xi(\text{min})},\;\sigma_{\xi(\text{max})}</annotation></semantics></math> are utilized as bounds by which observed state Gaussian parameters <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\text{peak}},\sigma_{\text{peak}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mtext>peak</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>peak</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\text{peak}},\sigma_{\text{peak}}</annotation></semantics></math> are uniformly sampled. These parameters were sampled to model the variation in sensor performance that could occur in the utilization of multiple sensors with varying noise characteristics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Sun (2019)</a></cite>. In all previous analyses, we explicitly set <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{\text{max}}=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mtext>max</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{\text{max}}=5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{total}}=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{total}}=100</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{\text{motion}}=10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mtext>motion</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{\text{motion}}=10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Explicitly-defined bounds for <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. <math id="S7.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\xi(\text{min})},\;\mu_{\xi(\text{max})},\sigma_{\xi(\text{min})},\;\sigma_{\xi(\text{max})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>min</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>max</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>min</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>max</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\xi(\text{min})},\;\mu_{\xi(\text{max})},\sigma_{\xi(\text{min})},\;\sigma_{\xi(\text{max})}</annotation></semantics></math>) are summarized in Table <a href="#S7.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ 7.3 Noise Generation Implementation for 𝜉_𝑘 ‣ 7 Appendix ‣ Hybrid State Estimation of Uncertain Nonlinear Dynamics Using Neural Processes" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. The parameter values selected represent extremely noisy IMU (inertial measurement unit) sensors and are utilized primarily as a stress test to show how robust all approaches are in uncertain sensing regimes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park and Gao (2008)</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
